course: reflection
lesson: 16
title: Things I'm ashamed of: crowds, cruelty and coming clean
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Shame is the hot, sinking feeling that comes when you know you did something wrong and you can't stop seeing it: it plays at night like a clip on repeat, and it often grows the longer the thing stays hidden.
explain: Finn and his mates threw stones at an old man's shed and cracked the window; it felt like a laugh that day, but the man came out shaking, close to tears, and months later Finn still walks past and thinks: "He was old. He looked scared of US. What kind of person does that?"
explain: In a crowd people do things they would never do alone, because everyone thinks someone else is in charge; the frightened face keeps coming back because deep down Finn knows it was cruel, and that is his conscience working, not proof he is rotten all the way through.
explain: Shame can turn into making things right: Finn told his mum, knocked on the man's door with her, said sorry and helped pay for the glass, and the man shook his hand; "everyone did it" never made the clip stop, but owning up slowly did.
explain: The same pattern runs through the other stories: a hidden wrong gets heavier day by day, and telling the truth, saying sorry and fixing what can be fixed is what lets it go quiet.
fact: Shame is feeling bad about who you are after doing wrong; guilt is feeling bad about what you did; guilt that leads to fixing things is useful.
fact: In a group people often act worse than each would alone; this is sometimes called mob mentality, and "everyone did it" does not make it fine.
fact: A lie that blames someone innocent can get them punished; being "framed" means being blamed for something you did not do, and "setting the record straight" means telling the truth afterwards.
fact: Guilt about a hidden wrong usually grows each day it is not fixed; owning up is scary for a moment and then usually brings relief.
fact: Children sometimes see scary or grown-up things online by accident; it is not their fault, and telling a calm adult helps them understand it and set up safety filters.
fact: A safe secret is like a surprise party; an unsafe secret is one where someone is being hurt, and unsafe secrets must be told to a trusted adult even if you promised not to.
fact: A trusted adult is a grown-up you can talk to who will help, such as a parent, teacher, school nurse or coach.
fact: Praise or an award you did not earn rarely feels good, because you know the truth; honesty makes the next award feel real.
fact: Blaming someone who cannot speak up, like a pet or a baby, is extra unfair; a "scapegoat" is someone blamed for other people's mistakes.
fact: "Coming clean" or "owning up" means admitting what you did; "making amends" means doing something to fix the harm.
fact: Shouting back when told off is often about embarrassment; a later sorry or a good deed can fix it.
fact: Gossip spreads very fast; whoever starts a secret moving shares the blame even if others spread it further.
fact: "Finders keepers" is not true for things like phones; keeping something you could return is a kind of stealing, and lost property offices exist to return things.
fact: Feeling ashamed for weeks shows you have a working conscience; the cure is usually honesty and making things right, not punishing yourself forever.
exercise: Finn and his mates cracked an old man's shed window and ran. Why did he do something he would never do alone? | In a group, everyone thinks someone else is in charge | He secretly hated old men and wanted to scare one of them badly | Throwing stones is only wrong if you get caught | Groups always make people kinder and calmer
exercise: Months later Finn still sees the man's frightened face. What does that mostly show? | His conscience knows it was cruel and wants it put right | He is a bad person who can never ever change | The old man is putting some kind of curse on him every single night | He needs to throw more stones to feel brave again
exercise: What finally helped Finn's shame go quiet? | Telling Mum, saying sorry and helping pay for the glass | Telling himself again and again that everyone else did it too, so it was fine | Taking the long way round so he never sees the shed | Waiting until the man moves away from the street
exercise: Jess falsely said a classmate pushed her, and the girl missed golden time. What is the right move? | Tell the teacher the truth and say sorry to the girl | Keep quiet, because the girl has already been punished | Say the girl pushed someone else, so it evens out | Wait two weeks and hope everybody forgets about it
exercise: Ben clicked on a scary grown-up video by accident and is afraid to tell. What is true? | It is not his fault, and a calm adult can help and set up filters | He will be in trouble forever, so it is best kept a secret from every grown-up | He must watch it again until it stops being scary | Seeing it by accident means he did it on purpose
exercise: Mo's friend says his dad hits him and makes Mo promise not to tell. What should Mo do? | Tell a trusted adult, because it is an unsafe secret | Keep the promise, because promises always come first | Tell the other kids so everybody knows about it | Wait until his friend is grown up and ask again
exercise: What is the difference between a safe secret and an unsafe one? | A safe one is like a surprise party; an unsafe one hides someone being hurt | A safe one is short; an unsafe one is long and hard to remember | A safe one is told to friends; an unsafe one is told only to pets | There is no difference at all, because every secret must be kept exactly the same
exercise: Grace got the reading award for reading she never did. Why did it feel fake? | She knew she had not earned it, so the praise felt hollow | Awards always feel bad, even when you have really earned them yourself | She was worried the certificate was printed wrongly | Nobody clapped loudly enough in the assembly hall
exercise: Harry broke his brother's console and blamed the dog, who was shut out in the rain. Why is that extra unfair? | The dog cannot speak up for itself | Dogs do not mind being blamed for things | It is only unfair if the console was new | Blaming a pet is fine if you give it treats
exercise: What helped Harry most? | Telling his brother, saying sorry and paying towards a fix | Giving the dog extra treats so he felt less guilty | Hiding the broken console under his bed for a week so nobody saw it | Buying himself a new game to cheer himself up
exercise: Lucy ate her sister's birthday chocolates and said "Wasn't me." What does "make amends" mean here? | Do something to fix it, like buying new ones and a real sorry | Pretend it never happened, so that nobody in the family gets more upset | Blame somebody else so that the family can move on | Wait until her sister's next birthday to say sorry
exercise: Archie shouted "Mind your own business!" at a man who told him off for litter. Why did he shout? | He was embarrassed at being caught | The man was wrong and litter does not matter | Shouting is the polite reply to any grown-up | He wanted to show he was the best at litter
exercise: Amira told her friend's crush to one girl, and by lunch everyone knew. What is true? | She started it moving, so she shares the blame | Only the girl who blabbed is to blame at all | Nobody is to blame, gossip spreads on its own | Her friend should never have had a crush on anybody in the first place
exercise: Tom found a phone on the bus and kept it. Is "finders keepers" right? | No; keeping what you could return is a kind of stealing | Yes; anything found on a bus belongs to whoever is lucky enough to find it | Yes, as long as he does not look at the photos | No, but only if the phone is a very expensive one
exercise: How did Tom feel after handing the phone in to the bus office? | Light and relieved | Angry, because he lost a phone that was his | Exactly the same as when he was using it | Scared that the owner would come and shout
exercise: Over weeks, what usually happens to a hidden wrong that nobody fixes? | The guilt tends to grow heavier day by day | It disappears completely after the first night | It turns into pride if you hide it well enough | It moves over onto whoever got blamed for it
test: Finn's mate goes, "it was just a laugh, bruv, everyone was doing it, chill." ("Bruv" means mate.) What's the best answer? | Everyone doing it doesn't make it okay; the man was really scared | He's right; if a whole group does it, nobody did anything wrong | He's right; old men don't really get scared of kids | It's fine as long as nobody was caught that day
test: Jess thinks: "If I tell the truth now, Miss will hate me forever." What usually happens? | Teachers mostly respect someone who comes back and owns up | The teacher will punish her twice as hard as the other girl | The teacher will not remember who said what anyway | Owning up late is worse than never owning up at all
test: Ben's cousin says, "don't tell your mum, she'll take your tablet off you, no cap." ("No cap" means honestly, no lie.) What is the wiser view? | Tell; it wasn't his fault and a calm adult can help | Keep it secret; parents always overreact to everything | Watch more videos so the scary one feels normal | Tell only his friends, because adults never understand
test: What would happen if Mo kept his promise and told nobody? | His friend could keep getting hurt with no grown-up to help | His friend's dad would stop hitting by himself after a while, without anyone knowing | Mo would feel completely calm and sleep soundly | The school would find out on its own the next day
test: Mo's friend is angry with him at first for telling. A few weeks later the friend says thanks. What does this show? | Telling an unsafe secret can be right even if a friend is upset at first | Friends always stay angry forever after you tell anybody anything they said | Mo should have told the whole class, not just a teacher | His friend was never really being hurt in the first place
test: Lass from Leeds says Grace "looked proper mardy after that award." ("Mardy" means sulky, grumpy.) Why might Grace be grumpy after winning? | She felt like a fraud because she hadn't done the reading | She wanted a bigger trophy instead of a certificate | She hates assemblies and everyone clapping at her | She thinks reading every night is a waste of time
test: In a cartoon, a boy blames the dog for a broken vase and everyone laughs, the end. What's missing from real life? | In real life the guilt usually keeps nagging until he owns up | Nothing; in real life blaming the dog makes the guilt vanish straight away | In real life dogs can tell the parents what happened | In real life vases never break, so it could not happen
test: Lucy's big sister snaps, "over CHOCOLATE? that's proper low." ("Low" here means mean, shabby.) What would fix it best? | A real sorry and replacing the chocolates | Saying sorry but still pretending it wasn't her | Eating the rest so there is nothing to argue about | Staying quiet for three more days until it blows over
test: Archie avoids the park for weeks after mouthing off to the man. What helped him more? | Going back, picking up litter and saying sorry | Finding a new park far away to play in | Telling everyone at school that the man had been really rude to him first | Never thinking about the park again
test: A kid says: "Amira didn't blab it to everybody, so it's not her fault at all, innit." ("Innit" means isn't it, right?) Which is true? | She started the gossip moving, so part of it is hers | He's right, only the last one who tells is to blame | He's right, telling just one person never counts | It is all the friend's fault for having a crush
test: Tom's gran from Cork says, "Ah, yer man who lost that phone is probably in bits." ("Yer man" means that person; "in bits" means very upset.) What does she want Tom to see? | The owner is probably upset, so the phone should go back | The owner doesn't care, so Tom should keep the phone | The phone is broken into bits and is worth nothing | Tom should sell the phone and then give all of the money away to charity
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises conduct within a group, as distinct from conduct of an individual alone? | Members of a group may act worse than each would individually | Groups invariably behave more kindly than their individual members | The conduct of a group is never attributable to any of its members | Persons in a group are incapable of experiencing subsequent shame
exam: It is given that a child has been entrusted with a secret concerning the physical harm of another child. Identify the correct course of action. | The matter shall be disclosed to a trusted adult notwithstanding the promise | The promise shall be honoured in all circumstances whatsoever | The matter shall be circulated among classmates for their opinion | The child shall refrain from any action pending the attainment by the friend of adulthood
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the retention of a mobile telephone found upon public transport. | Retention of property capable of return constitutes a form of theft | Property found upon a bus passes lawfully to the finder thereof | Retention is permissible provided the device remains unused | The owner of such property is deemed to have abandoned it
exam: In respect of guilt arising from a concealed wrongdoing, determine which statement is correct. | Such guilt commonly intensifies for so long as the wrong remains unremedied | Such guilt invariably dissipates within a single night | Concealment converts guilt into pride upon the expiry of a fixed period of time | Guilt is transferred in full to whosoever was wrongly blamed
